\section{Overview}

The memory subsystem provides the address bus abstraction for the Berzerk
emulator. Following MAME's design, all memory access goes through callbacks
rather than direct array indexing. This ensures observability and enables
instrumentation for debugging and cosimulation.

The Z80 CPU in Berzerk has two address spaces:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Program space}: 16-bit addresses (64~KB), used for ROM, RAM, VRAM,
  and memory-mapped hardware.
\item \textbf{I/O space}: 8-bit addresses (256 ports), used for sound, input,
  DIP switches, and control registers.
\end{itemize}

Each address range is mapped to a handler that defines the read and write
behavior. Some ranges are read-only (ROM), some are read-write (RAM, VRAM),
and some have side effects on read or write (Magic RAM, I/O ports).

