\section{Memory Handler Types}

A memory handler is a pair of callbacks: one for reads, one for writes. The
read callback takes an address and returns a byte. The write callback takes
an address and a byte. This follows MAME's [[data_read]]/[[data_write]] pattern.

Handlers that do nothing (unmapped regions) return 0xFF on read and silently
discard writes. This matches real Z80 bus behavior: floating data lines tend
to read high.

<<memory handler types>>=
using ReadHandler = std::function<uint8_t(uint16_t addr)>;
using WriteHandler = std::function<void(uint16_t addr, uint8_t data)>;

struct MemoryRegion {
    uint16_t start;
    uint16_t end;       // inclusive
    ReadHandler read;
    WriteHandler write;
    std::string name;   // for debug/instrumentation
};
@

\section{Address Space}

The [[AddressSpace]] class manages a list of memory regions. On each read or
write, it searches for the region that contains the target address and
dispatches to its handler.

The Berzerk machine has a simple memory map with no bank switching, so a
linear search through a small number of regions is adequate. MAME uses a
more complex page-table approach for machines with hundreds of regions, but
Berzerk has fewer than 10 program-space regions and fewer than 20 I/O
regions. Linear search over these is effectively free.

<<address space class>>=
class AddressSpace {
public:
    AddressSpace(const std::string& name, uint32_t addr_mask);

    // Register a memory region with read/write handlers.
    void map(uint16_t start, uint16_t end,
             ReadHandler read, WriteHandler write,
             const std::string& region_name);

    // Map a read-only region (writes are ignored).
    void map_rom(uint16_t start, uint16_t end,
                 ReadHandler read,
                 const std::string& region_name);

    // Map a region that returns a fixed value on read, ignores writes.
    void map_unconnected(uint16_t start, uint16_t end,
                         const std::string& region_name);

    uint8_t read(uint16_t addr) const;
    void write(uint16_t addr, uint8_t data);

    const std::string& name() const { return m_name; }

    // Instrumentation: count of reads and writes since last reset.
    uint64_t read_count() const { return m_read_count; }
    uint64_t write_count() const { return m_write_count; }
    void reset_counts() { m_read_count = 0; m_write_count = 0; }

private:
    std::string m_name;
    uint32_t m_addr_mask;
    std::vector<MemoryRegion> m_regions;
    mutable uint64_t m_read_count = 0;
    mutable uint64_t m_write_count = 0;
};
@

\section{ROM Descriptor and Loader}

Each Berzerk ROM file has a known filename, load address, size, and CRC32
checksum. The loader reads each file from disk and verifies its CRC32 against
the expected value. If a ROM fails verification, the loader reports the error
and returns failure rather than silently running with corrupt data.

CRC32 is computed using the standard polynomial 0xEDB88320 (reflected).
This matches the CRC values published in the MAME ROM definitions.

<<rom descriptor>>=
struct RomDescriptor {
    std::string filename;
    uint16_t load_addr;
    uint16_t size;
    uint32_t crc32;
};
@

<<rom loader declaration>>=
// Load ROM files into a flat byte array. Returns true on success.
// On failure, prints diagnostic to stderr and returns false.
bool load_roms(const std::string& rom_dir,
               const std::vector<RomDescriptor>& descriptors,
               std::vector<uint8_t>& rom_data,
               uint32_t rom_data_size);

// Compute CRC32 of a byte buffer.
uint32_t compute_crc32(const uint8_t* data, size_t length);
@

<<berzerk memory struct>>=
struct BerzerkMemory {
    AddressSpace program;
    AddressSpace io;
    std::vector<uint8_t> program_rom;
    std::vector<uint8_t> voice_rom;

    // Hardware state -- owned here for testability and cosimulation
    std::array<uint8_t, 0x0400> work_ram;
    VideoState video;
    InterruptState interrupts;
    SoundState sound;
    InputState input;

    BerzerkMemory() : program("program", 0xFFFF), io("io", 0xFF) {
        work_ram.fill(0);
    }
};

bool setup_berzerk_memory(BerzerkMemory& mem, const std::string& rom_dir);
@

